</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">email: </span><a href="mailto:zhang_mingxing@mail.tsinghua.edu.cn">zhang_mingxing@mail.tsinghua.edu.cn</a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Serverless computing provides dynamic scalability, but its infrastructure overhead becomes a bottleneck for emerging workloads such as LLM agents, which exhibit unpredictable invocation patterns and variable resource demands.
Our analysis shows that for these agents, the cost of running on serverless platforms can reach up to 70% of the cost of LLM API calls. This finding motivates the need for a more efficient, high-density serverless platform.
We present <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TrEnv</span>, a co-designed serverless platform that supports both container- and VM-based environments, optimized for the unique demands of LLM agents. <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TrEnv</span> reduces startup latency and memory usage through repurposable sandboxes and memory templates, which enable fast reuse and restoration of execution environments. To further reduce overhead in VM-based agent workloads, <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TrEnv</span> leverages browser sharing and a page cache bypassing mechanism.
Evaluations show that <span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TrEnv</span> reduces P99 latency by up to 7<math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> and memory usage by 48% in container-based settings, and achieves up to 58% lower P99 latency and 61% memory savings for VM-based agents compared to state-of-the-art systems like E2B.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>Serverless, Agent, CXL, Cold Start
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1. </span>Motivation</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Agent workloads powered by large language models (LLMs) are rapidly emerging as a foundational paradigm for modern AI applications. These agents integrate LLMs with external tools, task planners, and memory modules to enable context-aware, goal-oriented tasks.
Their capabilities span a wide range of tasks, from question answering and data analysis to more complex workflows, such as automated research (e.g., DeepResearch <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">ChatGPT, 2025</a>)</cite>), website generation (e.g., Manus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>), and game development (e.g., MGX <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">mgx, 2025</a>)</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Like other modern cloud-based applications, agent workloads exhibit highly dynamic and event-driven invocation patterns. They are inherently heterogeneous, encompassing both lightweight, interactive applications (e.g., question-answering agents) and more complex, tool-intensive tasks (e.g., research assistants invoking browsers or search engines). This diversity imposes stringent performance and efficiency requirements on the underlying serving infrastructure:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For lightweight agent applications, minimizing end-to-end execution latency is critical. For instance, the Blackjack application completes its core task in just 4.1 seconds, meaning that any added overhead, such as cold-start delays, can severely degrade user experience.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For more complex agents, execution often involves waiting for LLM outputs or external API responses, resulting in low CPU utilization. However, due to extensive tool usage (e.g., browsers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Müller and Žunič, 2024</a>)</cite>, code interpreters <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">E2B, 2025</a>)</cite>), memory consumption remains high even during I/O-bound periods. To improve overall CPU utilization, these agents benefit from high-density deployment and aggressive resource overcommitment, making it crucial to reduce memory overhead and redundancy across concurrently running agent instances.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Given the dynamic nature and fluctuating resource demands of agent workloads, serverless computing presents a natural deployment choice. Serverless platforms offer fine-grained resource allocation and billing, enabling applications to scale elastically while maximizing efficiency. These advantages have led to the widespread adoption of serverless computing across major cloud providers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alibaba, 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Amazon, 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Google, 2025b</a>)</cite> and various application domains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Amazon, 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ao et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carreira et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jonas et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pu et al., 2019</a>)</cite>.

Despite the utopian vision of serverless computing, its real-world implementations face many practical limitations.
Although serverless models suggest that functions could be transparently scheduled across data centers to maximize resource utilization, this idealized elasticity is often compromised by the functions’ need for efficient access to local and dedicated background environments.
This contradiction between computational elasticity and environment localization poses challenges to the realization of serverless computing’s full potential.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Precisely, the execution of a serverless function typically unfolds in three phases.</p>
<ol id="S1.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The sandbox creation phase establishes an isolated sandbox, e.g., a container or virtual machine (VM);</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The bootstrapping phase initializes the function, which may involve steps like launching Python/Java virtual machines;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, the execution phase executes instance-specific logic according to the inputs of this invocation.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">The first two phases are essential for setting up an execution environment for each function invocation. However, they do not directly contribute to processing user inputs, thus their overheads (in both CPU time and memory consumption) should be minimized.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Unfortunately, studies show that the time required to create such dedicated environments can significantly exceed the actual execution time of a function <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Du et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Silva et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, leading to the notorious “cold start” problem in serverless computing.
The requirement for execution environments also gives rise to resource utilization issues, such as “memory stranding” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and “state duplication” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Saxena et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.
Memory stranding occurs when a server’s computational resources are fully engaged, but its memory remains underutilized.
State duplication arises when concurrent serverless functions require but cannot efficiently share identical states due to existing isolation mechanisms.
Recent studies have indicated that these challenges lead to significant memory inefficiencies, with up to 50% of memory resources being underutilized in the cloud <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fuerst et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Javadi et al., 2019</a>)</cite> and an 80% occurrence of state duplication <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Saxena et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">These limitations collectively hinder the elastic scaling of applications on existing serverless platforms.

Our further measurements indicate that infrastructure overhead, i.e., the cost incurred by the serverless platform, can account for more than 40% of the total cost of running typical agent applications, comparable to the cost of LLM API calls.
This underscores the urgent need for serverless platforms to evolve in response to the unique requirements of serverless workloads including LLM-centric agents, in order to improve cost efficiency and scalability.</p>
</div>
