</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2. </span>Contribution</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To address these challenges, we propose <span id="S1.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TrEnv</span>, an optimized platform tailored for agent and serverless workloads that crafted to minimize CPU time and memory costs associated with the execution environments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Existing serverless platforms generally fall into two categories:</p>
<ol id="S1.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Container-based platforms (e.g., OpenFaaS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenFaaS, 2025b</a>)</cite>), which utilize Linux namespaces and cgroups for lightweight isolation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">VM-based platforms (e.g., Cloud Hypervisor <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Intel, 2025</a>)</cite>), , which offer stronger isolation at the cost of higher startup overhead. These are commonly adopted by major public cloud providers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agache et al., 2020</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Considering the existing two types of serverless platform, <span id="S1.SS2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TrEnv</span> introduces targeted optimizations for container- and VM-based platforms to improve startup efficiency and reduce memory overhead, thereby enabling high-density, high-performance execution of serverless and agent applications.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The main idea is to share components of these environments across different functions and nodes as much as possible.
Rather than discarding this execution environment upon the completion of a function instance, <span id="S1.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TrEnv</span> cleanses and reallocates it to a repurposable sandbox pool.
Subsequent functions can reuse these sandboxes by attaching function-specific memory states, which are offloaded to a shared CXL or RDMA-based memory pool.
Through innovative enhancements at the operating system (OS), hypervisor and core libraries like Checkpoint/Restore In Userspace (CRIU) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">CRIU, 2025</a>)</cite>,
this procedure typically takes less than 10 ms for containers and 40 ms for VMs.
Only updated (writable) memory pages are instantiated per invocation, while read-only pages are transparently shared across multiple instances and nodes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TrEnv</span> ’s approach of container repurposing, while bearing similarities to existing caching solutions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shahrad et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fuerst and Sharma, 2021</a>)</cite>, distinguishes itself in two significant ways.
First, <span id="S1.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TrEnv</span> enables the transparent reuse of sandboxes not only across invocations of the same function, but also across different function types, whether the function executes directly in a container or runs within a VM sandbox (sometimes referred to as a “jailer” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agache et al., 2020</a>)</cite>).
Both runtime environments rely on Linux isolation primitives (e.g., namespaces, cgroups). <span id="S1.SS2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TrEnv</span> performs a systematic decomposition of sandbox creation overhead to determine which components are safely reusable and which require lightweight reconfiguration.
Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1.1. Motivation ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ TrEnv: Transparently Share Serverless Execution Environments Across Different Functions and Nodes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> outlines these components, with further discussions in §<a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1. Challenge on Reusing Isolated Sandbox ‣ 4. Overview of the Container-Based Platform ‣ TrEnv: Transparently Share Serverless Execution Environments Across Different Functions and Nodes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, §<a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2. Repurpose Isolated Sandbox ‣ 5. Design of Container-based Platform ‣ TrEnv: Transparently Share Serverless Execution Environments Across Different Functions and Nodes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> and §<a href="#S8.SS1" title="8.1. Security ‣ 8. Discussion ‣ TrEnv: Transparently Share Serverless Execution Environments Across Different Functions and Nodes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Secondly, <span id="S1.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TrEnv</span> offloads function-specific memory states to a shared memory pool. This strategy shares the states across multiple nodes, effectively amortizing the overall expense.
However, operating system modifications are required to rapidly instantiate serverless functions from snapshots stored in remote memory pools.
This process resembles performing a “fork” but includes capabilities beyond those offered by current operating systems. To support this, we have extended the kernel with an <em id="S1.SS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mm-template API</em>, enabling the creation of custom memory templates for each serverless function.
These templates consist of reserved page tables that map to remote, potentially overlapping, non-contiguous memory segments within the pool.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.09525v1/pool-type.svg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:191/106;" width="191" height="106"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>. </span><span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Multi-layer architecture of mm-template.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The flexibility of our design is one of its key strengths. As demonstrated in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1.2. Contribution ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ TrEnv: Transparently Share Serverless Execution Environments Across Different Functions and Nodes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, <span id="S1.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TrEnv</span> integrates mm-templates with both CXL and RDMA memory pools, each offering distinct advantages.
Notably, to leverage the byte-addressable capabilities of CXL, mm-templates enable direct reads for read-only pages, which introduces zero additional software-level overhead during execution.
Given our analysis showing that 24% to 90% of memory accesses are to read-only pages in serverless functions, this technique substantially accelerates processing times by avoiding unnecessary data copying or page faults, thereby significantly reducing latency.
Despite this, existing OS primarily utilizes CXL for memory expansion as per the capabilities of CXL 1.1, thus lacking support for memory sharing and deduplication required in multi-node environments introduced since CXL 2.0.
This limitation hinders us from achieving transparent memory state sharing in container-based platforms, especially when dealing with memory areas that grow dynamically, e.g., heaps.
We have enhanced the OS to allow precise control over the mapping between virtual and physical addresses in CXL memory and supported both file-backed and anonymous memory mappings to maintain a process’s complete state.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Different from CXL, integrating RDMA-based remote memory follows a lazy paging strategy, where any access to remote memory triggers a major page fault that fetches a 4KB block online.
Since all states in the memory pool are read-only, with write operations managed through copy-on-write mechanisms, a multi-layered architecture integrates seamlessly with our approach.
This allows for the strategic placement of hot pages in the upper layers, such as CXL or even local memory, and cold pages in the lower layers, such as RDMA or even network-attached storage.
The specific number of layers, as well as the cache eviction or page promotion strategies, are orthogonal to our core implementation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Additionally, RunD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022a</a>)</cite> previously proposed a container rootfs mapping scheme that reduces page cache duplication and improves density for VM-based platforms. However, its method breaks the copy-on-write (CoW) semantics, making it incompatible with safe memory state sharing across nodes. To address this, <span id="S1.SS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TrEnv</span> combines virtio pmem devices with the mm-template API, enabling fast VM startup, state sharing, and page cache deduplication simultaneously.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">This paper makes the following key contributions:</p>
<ul id="S1.I4" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We analyze the performance bottlenecks and characteristics of agent and serverless applications on both container- and VM-based platforms.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a repurposable sandbox design that enables transparent sharing across heterogeneous function types, reducing per-invocation overhead.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We design and implement the mm-template API, which facilitates remote memory state sharing with minimal restoration latency and reduced memory duplication.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I4.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I4.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I4.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce two optimizations for agent applications on VM-based platforms: (1) extending our sharing mechanism to browser processes to reduce execution latency, and (2) integrating virtio-pmem with mm-template to enable memory state sharing while mitigating page cache duplication.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I4.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I4.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I4.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We develop <span id="S1.I4.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TrEnv</span>, a serverless platform built atop faasd <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenFaaS, 2025a</a>)</cite> and Cloud Hypervisor <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Intel, 2025</a>)</cite>, supporting both container and VM runtimes.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I4.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I4.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I4.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our evaluation on container-based workloads demonstrates that:
(1) For prior serverless workloads, <span id="S1.I4.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TrEnv</span> achieves up to <math id="S1.I4.i6.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="7\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7\times</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.I4.i6.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="16\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16\times</annotation></semantics></math> speedup in P99 end-to-end latency under concurrent loads compared with REAP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib85" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ustiugov et al., 2021</a>)</cite> and FaaSnap <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ao et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, two state-of-the-art (SOTA) lazy restoration methods, respectively; <span id="S1.I4.i6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TrEnv</span> reduces peak memory usage by 48% on average;
(2) A detailed comparison between real-world CXL and RDMA memory pools reveals that CXL provides more stable performance, particularly at P99, and offers up to a <math id="S1.I4.i6.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="3.51\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.51</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.51\times</annotation></semantics></math> speedup on execution time over RDMA.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I4.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I4.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I4.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our evaluation on VM-based agent workloads demonstrates <span id="S1.I4.i7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TrEnv</span> achieves at most 61% memory savings and 58% P99 latency reduction compared with the SOTA system, E2B, after adopting our optimizations, while keeping the same efficient startup latency.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Case Study: Agent</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Basics of Agents.</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated substantial capabilities across a range of tasks, including natural language understanding and content generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
These advances have catalyzed the emergence of a new class of LLM-driven applications for autonomous task completion, commonly referred to as agents (e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">LangChain, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hong et al., 2024</a>)</cite>).
An agent is a system or program that leverages an LLM to solve user-defined tasks, incorporating capabilities such as reasoning, planning, and external tool usage.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.T2" class="ltx_table">

<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S2.T2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 2</span>. </span><span id="S2.T2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The characteristic of representative agents evaluated on Firecracker.</span></figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
